  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/mesonauta_acora_tocantins_en/",
      "html": "<p>M. acora was described from the Rio Araguaia, a large tributary of the Rio Tocantins in Brazil. The imported fish come from the Rio Tocantins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/mesonauta_acora_tocantins_en/",
      "html": "<p>Flag cichlids (Mesonauta) from the Rio Tocantins in Brazil have a very variable basic pattern. This dark pattern, shown mainly when the fish are stressed or excited, is usually species-specific. Here, however, almost all specimens would belong to different species, which is not likely at all.</p><p>An examination of the described Mesonauta species makes it very likely that these imports belong to Mesonauta acora, a species known for its variable basic colours, and the stated distribution fits.</p><p>According to the revision of Mesonauta by Kullander and Silfvergrip, M. acora has a unique feature: 2-4 scales (most often 3) on the preoperculum. This is not visible to the naked eye in a living specimen, but can be seen on a photo.</p><p>M. acora has not been imported before, or at least the identification was not certain. It is said to be the rarest flag cichlid species in the hobby.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/mesonauta_acora_tocantins_en/",
      "html": "<p>Kullander, S. O. &amp; A. M. C. Silfvergrip (1991): Review of the South American cichlid genus Mesonauta Günther (Teleostei, Cichlidae) with descriptions of two new species. Revue Suisse de Zoologie v. 98 (no. 2): 407-448.</p>"
    }
